# File granularity

Do not create a new file for a small piece of logic that has no reason to live independently.

Before creating a new file, ask in order:

1. Genuine runtime/deployment boundary (service, frontend app, worker/lambda)? → new module OK
2. Imported by three or more unrelated modules? → shared file OK
3. One cohesive domain concept a teammate would look for by name? → own file OK, but put ALL closely related functions for that concept in it

If all three are no — keep it in the most relevant existing file. A file that is "one small function used by one caller" must not exist.

Anti-patterns:
- One file per model when models form one domain → one schema module
- One file per route when routes share a resource → one router file
- Thin "service" wrappers around a single library call
- `utils/` full of one-function files → group by theme

Reverse failure: do not cram unrelated domains into one giant file. Target is cohesion, not line-count. When unsure, prefer fewer well-organized files.
